{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answers\":[{\"question_id\":\"Q002_mazg_hise_remote_homology\",\"answer\":\"SCOPe 2.08 places d1vmga_ (MazG-like; a.204.1.2) and d1y6xa1 (HisE-like/PRA-PH; a.204.1.4) in different families within the same all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases superfamily, a.204.1. This supports remote homology—a probable common evolutionary origin at superfamily level—but not a close, same-family relationship. It does not justify transferring function between them: d1vmga_ cannot thereby be called HisE or assigned phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase/histidine-biosynthesis activity, and d1y6xa1 cannot thereby be assigned MazG-like substrate specificity or physiological function. Shared reaction chemistry, substrates, catalytic residues, or biological roles require independent biochemical and sequence/active-site evidence.\",\"evidence_used\":[\"mcp__scope__get_scop_domains\",\"/api/v1/releases/current\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/homology\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/homology\"]}]}"}}
